= 2. Proposal Summary

Satellite fire records are among the longest and most consistent sources of information about where and when vegetation burns. NASA FIRMS distributes active-fire detections from the Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) since November 2000 and from the Visible Infrared Imaging Radiometer Suite (VIIRS) since 2012 @firmsarchive. The two sensors observe at different resolutions and times of day, and VIIRS detects far more small fires @firmsmodis @firmsviirs. When the records are simply added together, every year after 2012 appears to have several times more fire than the years before it, even though the change comes from the instruments and not from the landscape.

The team built PyroTrace to address this problem for Central America. The platform downloads the complete FIRMS archive for Belize, Costa Rica, El Salvador, Guatemala, Honduras, Nicaragua, and Panama, and harmonizes it with a four-stage method that keeps every original detection and assigns it a weight in VIIRS-equivalent units. A MODIS detection that VIIRS also observed receives no weight, because the fire is already counted. A MODIS detection without a VIIRS match receives a factor estimated for its country and time of day. The weighted detections are stored in a spatial database and published through a web application with an interactive map, a historical calendar, and a statistics panel that compares any month or year with its historical range.

The main result is a harmonized record of 3,621,309 detections from November 2000 to December 2025. Between 2011 and 2013, raw detections grow by a factor of 6.41, whereas the harmonized index grows by a factor of 1.57. The method therefore removes most of the artificial jump at the start of VIIRS. A consistency check against MODIS shows that a residual step of about 37% remains, which defines the next calibration task. PyroTrace makes this evidence visible instead of hiding it: users can switch between raw counts and the harmonized index, read where each value comes from, and download the underlying detections.
